/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), browsing-topics=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' https://static.ads-twitter.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://t.co https://*.twitter.com https://*.x.com; connect-src 'self' https://t.co https://*.twitter.com https://*.x.com https://*.ads-twitter.com; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# The only third-party origins allowed are X's ad tag (static.ads-twitter.com) and the
# endpoints it reports to. It loads only after cookie consent (src/components/Consent.astro).
# Anything else third-party is a decision to make, not a header to loosen.
#
# Two directives need explaining.
#
# script-src: the inline-script hashes are NOT listed above. Astro inlines small scripts into the
# HTML instead of emitting files, so 'self' alone blocks the theme toggle, the table's
# search and sort, and the calculator. `npm run build` runs scripts/csp-hashes.ts, which
# reads the real build output and inserts a sha256 hash per inline script straight after
# 'self' in dist/_headers. Host sources written here are kept.
#
# style-src carries 'unsafe-inline' because the pages use inline style attributes (the
# table's per-row animation delay, for one) and CSP hashes cannot cover style attributes
# at all — only <style> elements. On a static site with no user-supplied content this is
# a small exposure, and it buys back the ability to keep script-src genuinely strict,
# which is where cross-site scripting actually lives.
